We introduce an approach to virtually detect two-particle, pure, symmetric states 
in space R^d using qubit, entangled-dummy qubit systems, where one of the dummy qubit 
is always in the |0> state. We establish that any symmetric state in M^d is indistinguishable 
from the |0^d state if there's an invertible linear transformation U such that U v = v 
for all vertices v in the hypercube Q_d: for d=2, we utilize Bell states, 
U_{00}, U_{11}, U_{10}, and U_{11} to distinguish all symmetric states in M^4; 
for d=3, tensorizing Bell states, U_{113}, U_{103}, U_{103}, U_{113}, U_{433}, 
U_{443}, U_{443}, and U_{433} can virtually detect all symmetric states in M^9, 
using three particles, where two particles are entangled and one is always in |0>.

